 "notes": "Curve is the EDELWEISS-III bound on the ALP dark-matter absorption coupling to electrons g_ae vs ALP mass, Fig. 5 left (alp_dm.png), the thick red solid line (EDELWEISS-II is the red dashed line above it). g_ae here is already the canonical dimensionless axion-electron coupling. 90% C.L. Direct DM absorption search; paper assumes local DM density rho_DM=0.3 GeV/cm^3 (Eq.3), not rescaled. Extracted by pixel analysis of the raster figure: x-axis 1keV@col392.5,10keV@695,100keV@999.5 (~303.5 px/decade); y-axis 1e-10@row21,1e-14@row736. Per column I took the bottommost bright-red run (EDELWEISS-III lies below EDELWEISS-II wherever both appear), then per log-mass bin took the median, tracing through the dense radioactive-line forest (peaks at K/L-shell energies ~10keV). Clipped to the papers stated range >0.8 keV (upper edge ~400-500 keV). The forest oscillates by a factor of a few between line peaks and troughs, so localized values are uncertain though the overall rising trend is robust. Paper also reports other limits not chosen here: solar-axion CBRD g_ae<1.1e-11 (Fig.3, massless limit), 57Fe combination g_ae*g_aN^eff<3.5e-17 (Fig.4), and a hidden-photon DM kinetic-mixing chi limit (Fig.5 right). The ALP-electron DM absorption curve was selected as the main mass-dependent AxionElectron result.",
